= Classification — Technical =

== Classification function ==
A single-label classifier can be modeled:

<pre>
f : X → C
</pre>

mapping an input x to category c.

Multi-label classification instead maps:

<pre>
f : X → P(C)
</pre>

where P(C) is a set of category subsets.

== Confusion matrix ==
For binary classification:

<pre>
true positive
false positive
true negative
false negative
</pre>

support metrics such as precision, recall and specificity.

These statistical meanings of precision should not be confused with metrological [[Accuracy and precision/Technical|precision]].

== Distribution shift ==
A classifier learned under distribution P(X,Y) may fail when deployment distribution Q(X,Y) differs.

This separates:
* model performance;
* category validity;
* environment stability.

== Ontology classification ==
In description logic, classification computes subsumption relations among named classes.

If ontology O entails:

<pre>
A ⊑ B
</pre>

a reasoner places A below B in the inferred hierarchy.

This is logical classification, not statistical prediction.

== SKOS organization ==
SKOS broader/narrower relationships form a concept organization scheme.

They are intentionally weaker than strict class subsumption and should not be fed into DL classification as if they were equivalent without an explicit mapping.

